With package management and versioned releases established, the next critical enterprise pillar 
is Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).

In production, engineering teams, automated CI/CD runners (like GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, or Jenkins),
 and pods must never operate with cluster-admin privileges. Kubernetes uses RBAC to enforce the Principle of Least Privilege.


1. The RBAC Trinity: Who --> What --> How

Every RBAC policy in Kubernetes answers one simple question: 
Can [SUBJECT] perform [VERB] on [RESOURCE] in [NAMESPACE]?

+------------------+         +----------------------+         +-----------------------+
|     SUBJECT      |         |     BINDING          |         |         ROLE          |
|  (Who is asking) |         | (The glue attaching  |         | (What actions/verbs   |
|                  |  ===>   |   Subject to Role)   |  ===>   |  are permitted)       |
| - ServiceAccount |         |                      |         |                       |
| - User / Group   |         | - RoleBinding        |         | - Role (Namespace)    |
+------------------+         | - ClusterRoleBinding |         | - ClusterRole (Global)|
                             +----------------------+         +-----------------------+

A. Subjects: Who is making the API request?

Humans (Users & Groups): Kubernetes does not manage human user accounts in etcd.
 Authentication is delegated to external identity providers (OIDC, AWS IAM, Azure AD, Okta, or X.509 
 client certificates).

Workloads & Automations (ServiceAccount): Managed directly inside Kubernetes. ServiceAccounts 
 are assigned to Pods or CI/CD pipelines so processes can authenticate against kube-apiserver.

B. Roles: What can be done?

A Role defines a whitelist of allowed permissions:

Role: Confined to a single Namespace (e.g., can only view pods in staging).

ClusterRole: Cluster-wide (e.g., can view Nodes, PersistentVolumes, or pods across all namespaces).

Permissions are defined by three fields:

apiGroups: Which API group owns the resource (e.g., "" for core resources like Pods/Services, "apps" 
 for Deployments/StatefulSets).

resources: The targets (["pods", "deployments", "configmaps"]).

verbs: Allowed actions (["get", "list", "watch", "create", "update", "patch", "delete"]).

C. Bindings: Connecting the two

RoleBinding: Grants permissions within a specific namespace.

ClusterRoleBinding: Grants permissions cluster-wide.

